  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.25 × loss. It is 4 times steeper in x, so overshooting starts there: with no noise, x swings grow once the learning rate is above 0.5. Each step moves −learning rate × (true slope + noise).",
    "The noise is Gaussian and centred on zero. Its spread is 3·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, like sampling b of 64 points without replacement, so it is exactly 0 at 64. The values come from fixed seed 7 and depend on the run and step number.",
    "Both arrows use the same scale, 2 × the step length. Orange is the actual next step against the noisy slope. Grey is the step the full-data slope would give. When the run is over, both are hidden and the step length shows 0.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as soon as the loss passes 10, the bowl's highest drawn corner (diverged). A ball that leaves the drawn bowl is shown at its edge, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus up to 40 landing points. 'New run, keep last path' saves the finished path as the ghost and moves on to the next noise values. The card's Reset clears both paths and replays the noise from the start."
  ]
}